WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.765
Day 10 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.315 --> 00:00:09.182
Playwright MCP: an agent that browses your app and proposes tests.

00:00:09.732 --> 00:00:14.828
Connect Playwright MCP to your agent, run a guided exploration and end with a test

00:00:14.828 --> 00:00:17.874
plan and one test built on the page's real roles.

00:00:19.174 --> 00:00:24.275
For years, generating UI tests with AI meant pasting HTML into a chat and getting

00:00:24.275 --> 00:00:25.724
guessed selectors back.

00:00:26.024 --> 00:00:30.713
The model never saw the application running: it didn't know what appears after a

00:00:30.713 --> 00:00:34.816
click, which button ends up disabled or how long a list takes to load.

00:00:35.116 --> 00:00:38.648
The resulting tests compiled and failed on the first run.

00:00:39.948 --> 00:00:44.959
The Playwright MCP server changes that starting point: it gives the agent a browser

00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:45.744
it can drive.

00:00:46.044 --> 00:00:50.702
It navigates, clicks, fills in forms and reads the real state of the page.

00:00:51.002 --> 00:00:55.550
This guide shows how to use it in order, first to explore and only at the end to

00:00:55.550 --> 00:01:00.155
write code, and which limits to set, because an agent that reads pages also reads

00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:01.918
whatever someone wrote on them.

00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:04.916
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:05.466 --> 00:01:09.183
Node.js 22+ and an MCP-capable agent.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:15.529
Practice app: https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc.

00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:18.625
Test environments and test accounts only.

00:01:19.925 --> 00:01:21.204
This is the setup.

00:01:21.504 --> 00:01:25.721
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:29.288
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:30.588 --> 00:01:31.624
Step 1.

00:01:31.924 --> 00:01:33.506
Connect and confirm.

00:01:34.056 --> 00:01:39.266
Connect the Playwright MCP server to your agent and confirm the browser tools show

00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:39.457
up.

00:01:40.007 --> 00:01:44.678
Add the server to your agent with the command in the guide and, before asking for

00:01:44.678 --> 00:01:46.697
anything, list the available tools.

00:01:46.997 --> 00:01:52.270
The browser ones must show up: navigate, click, type, take a snapshot.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:57.249
If they don't, the agent will answer anyway, but from memory, and you'll believe it

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:59.109
explored the page when it didn't.

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:02.756
That ten-second check prevents the most common confusion.

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:07.661
Then ask for something trivial, such as opening a URL and stating the title, to

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:09.351
confirm the browser responds.

00:02:10.651 --> 00:02:11.849
Step 2.

00:02:12.149 --> 00:02:13.603
What the agent sees.

00:02:14.153 --> 00:02:19.780
Understand what the agent sees: an accessibility snapshot with roles and names, not

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:20.391
an image.

00:02:20.691 --> 00:02:23.062
That's why its locators come out better.

00:02:23.612 --> 00:02:28.214
It doesn't see a screenshot but an accessibility snapshot: a text tree with each

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:31.091
element's role, its accessible name and its state.

00:02:31.391 --> 00:02:36.258
A button appears as a button with its label, a field as a textbox with its label.

00:02:36.558 --> 00:02:38.395
That has two consequences.

00:02:38.695 --> 00:02:44.123
First, the locators it proposes are based on roles and names, which are more stable

00:02:44.123 --> 00:02:45.234
than CSS classes.

00:02:45.534 --> 00:02:50.457
Second, a page with poor accessibility is as hard for it as for a screen reader, and

00:02:50.457 --> 00:02:51.980
that is already a finding.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:54.363
Step 3.

00:02:54.663 --> 00:02:56.639
Explore without writing code.

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.525
Explore first, no code.

00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:04.681
Use the exploration prompt and read the report of roles, states and possible

00:03:04.681 --> 00:03:05.192
defects.

00:03:05.742 --> 00:03:09.817
Ask it to walk through a flow and report what it finds, with the explicit

00:03:09.817 --> 00:03:11.770
instruction not to write tests yet.

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:17.151
The report should list elements by role, the states it observed, such as empty

00:03:17.151 --> 00:03:21.059
lists, errors or disabled elements, and whatever seemed odd.

00:03:21.359 --> 00:03:25.347
Read it the way you'd read the notes of someone using the product for the first

00:03:25.347 --> 00:03:25.599
time.

00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:29.083
It's useful for discovering states you hadn't thought of.

00:03:29.383 --> 00:03:33.913
It isn't useful as a bug list: the agent doesn't know your business rules.

00:03:35.213 --> 00:03:36.226
Step 4.

00:03:36.526 --> 00:03:39.164
A Markdown plan, corrected by you.

00:03:39.714 --> 00:03:44.539
Ask for a Markdown test plan based on what it observed and fix it yourself:

00:03:44.539 --> 00:03:46.404
priorities and missing cases.

00:03:46.954 --> 00:03:51.499
With exploration done, ask for a test plan in Markdown based only on what was

00:03:51.499 --> 00:03:52.030
observed.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.584
A plan is cheap to correct and a set of tests isn't.

00:03:55.884 --> 00:04:00.209
Review it with three questions: which cases are missing that only someone who knows

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.587
the business would know, which are redundant because they repeat the same thing, and

00:04:04.587 --> 00:04:06.568
what the real priority of each one is.

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:09.134
Save the corrected plan in the repo.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:14.063
It's the specification of what you'll ask for next, and it records what you decided

00:04:14.063 --> 00:04:15.625
to test and what you didn't.

00:04:16.925 --> 00:04:18.054
Step 5.

00:04:18.354 --> 00:04:19.842
One test at a time.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:25.736
Ask for a single test from the plan, using the roles it saw, and run it three times.

00:04:26.286 --> 00:04:31.496
Ask for a single test from the plan, stating that it must use the roles and names it

00:04:31.496 --> 00:04:32.488
saw on the page.

00:04:32.788 --> 00:04:36.169
Run it three times in a row before asking for the next one.

00:04:36.469 --> 00:04:41.123
Generating one at a time looks slow, but each passing test confirms the conventions

00:04:41.123 --> 00:04:45.272
were understood, and each failure gets fixed once instead of twenty times.

00:04:45.572 --> 00:04:50.381
When a test fails, first check whether the problem is the test or the application.

00:04:50.681 --> 00:04:55.607
If the agent explored and the test still fails, it may have found a real defect.

00:04:56.907 --> 00:04:58.094
Step 6.

00:04:58.394 --> 00:04:59.604
Safety limits.

00:05:00.154 --> 00:05:05.262
Set limits: never production or real credentials, and remember the agent reads all

00:05:05.262 --> 00:05:07.691
page content, including malicious text.

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.649
Two fixed rules.

00:05:09.949 --> 00:05:12.842
First: never production or real credentials.

00:05:13.142 --> 00:05:17.359
The agent can click anything it sees, including delete and pay buttons.

00:05:17.659 --> 00:05:23.225
Second: all page text enters its context, and that includes content written by third

00:05:23.225 --> 00:05:27.333
parties, such as comments, user names or product descriptions.

00:05:27.633 --> 00:05:31.606
If someone wrote an instruction there, the agent may try to follow it.

00:05:31.906 --> 00:05:36.685
Use a test environment with controlled data and review any action you didn't ask

00:05:36.685 --> 00:05:36.924
for.

00:05:38.224 --> 00:05:39.330
An example.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:45.049
A tester connects the server and asks the agent to explore a to-do list application.

00:05:45.349 --> 00:05:50.576
The report lists a text field, a checkbox per task, three filters and a counter.

00:05:50.876 --> 00:05:56.068
It flags two suspicions: the counter says "1 items left" with a single task, and

00:05:56.068 --> 00:05:58.599
there is no visible way to edit a task.

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:03.163
The first is a minor copy defect; the second isn't a defect, you edit by

00:06:03.163 --> 00:06:06.065
double-clicking and the agent didn't discover it.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:11.360
She corrects the plan by adding the edit case, asks for the first test, "add a

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.473
task", and it passes three times.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:17.596
In twenty minutes she has a reviewed plan and a trustworthy test.

00:06:18.896 --> 00:06:20.558
The most common mistakes.

00:06:21.108 --> 00:06:24.757
Asking for tests before exploring and having a reviewed plan.

00:06:25.307 --> 00:06:28.200
Not confirming that the browser tools are loaded.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:32.340
Treating the exploration report as a list of confirmed bugs.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:36.875
Pointing the agent at an environment with real data or credentials.

00:06:38.175 --> 00:06:40.419
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:40.969 --> 00:06:43.653
The browser tools show up in the agent's list.

00:06:44.203 --> 00:06:47.967
There is an exploration report with roles, states and suspicions.

00:06:48.517 --> 00:06:51.736
The Markdown plan is corrected and saved in the repo.

00:06:52.286 --> 00:06:55.470
One generated test passed three runs in a row.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:06:58.665
And where AI fell short.

00:06:59.215 --> 00:07:04.163
It confuses expected behavior with defects when it doesn't know the product rules.

00:07:04.463 --> 00:07:08.204
The exploration report is a list of suspicions, not bugs.

00:07:09.504 --> 00:07:10.889
That was day 10.

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:16.163
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:16.163 --> 00:07:17.170
asked questions.

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:23.103
Continue with day 11: Playwright Test Agents: planner, generator and healer in

00:07:23.103 --> 00:07:23.586
action.
